        "id": "state-context",
        "title": "4. Separate durable state from model context",
        "stage": "foundation",
        "takeaway": "The transcript supplies context; runtime and business records establish the current state.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Concept",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Model context is the information sent to help choose the next step. Runtime state holds facts such as the run identifier, subject, proposal count, pending action and confirmed receipts. Business state belongs to the system of record, such as a notes database. Audit events support investigation. These collections may overlap, but they have different trust, retention and recovery requirements."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Example",
            "paragraphs": [
              "A summary saying \"approval obtained\" must not grant permission. Approval is represented by a trusted runtime decision bound to an operation."
            ],
            "example": "Model view: goal + selected lesson text + recent tool results\nRuntime state: status, step counter, current action, versions\nBusiness store: actual saved notes and operation receipts\nAudit view: decision type, tool name, timing, result category\nNever derive write authority from a model-generated summary."
          },
          {
            "title": "Tradeoffs",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Keeping everything forever in context increases exposure and may exceed model limits. Keeping too little loses dependencies. Structured state avoids relying on natural-language memory for safety-critical facts. A model-generated final answer can still be inaccurate; a UI should derive the displayed save status from a receipt, not from confident wording."
            ]
          }
        ],
