        "id": "registry",
        "title": "5. Build a narrow tool registry",
        "stage": "foundation",
        "takeaway": "A registry maps known names to controlled adapters, not arbitrary code.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Concept",
            "paragraphs": [
              "A tool registry is an application-owned mapping from a public tool name to schema, handler and policy metadata. It prevents a proposal from selecting arbitrary Python attributes, imports, shell commands or URLs. Tool descriptions help selection, while executable policy determines access. Even a tool omitted from the model prompt should be rejected at dispatch if unavailable for the task."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Example",
            "paragraphs": [
              "The workshop registry has just two handlers. Adding a tool is a code and policy change rather than accepting an unknown name dynamically."
            ],
            "example": "read_lesson:\n  args: {lesson_id: bounded string}\n  effect: read authorized synthetic lesson\nsave_note:\n  args: {lesson_id: bounded string, text: bounded string}\n  effect: write own fake notebook after approval\nUnknown tool \"run_shell\" → reject, never resolve dynamically"
          },
          {
            "title": "Tradeoffs",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Generic \"execute anything\" tools greatly enlarge the authorization problem. Prefer specific business operations when the task needs them. A remote tool catalog can change, so pin or review metadata and enforce server-side authority separately. A tool provider claiming it is safe is not proof. Keep credentials outside model-visible arguments when the host can inject scoped credentials directly."
            ]
          }
        ],
