        "id": "capstone",
        "title": "24. Build and defend the offline harness",
        "stage": "advanced",
        "takeaway": "Demonstrate each policy with an observable fixture and name the limits.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Concept",
            "paragraphs": [
              "The downloadable workshop combines a scripted proposal source, a narrow registry, input validation, resource policy, scoped approval, cooperative limits, fake idempotent effects, JSON checkpointing and metadata events. Read it as a small reference implementation, then rebuild selected pieces. Its fake store is single-process and volatile. It is intentionally not an LLM agent, distributed scheduler, secure sandbox or production approval service."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Example",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Use an evidence table to distinguish implemented behavior from the architecture extensions you would need for deployment."
            ],
            "example": "Implemented: reject unknown tools → zero fake effects\nImplemented: changed approval intent → no dispatch\nImplemented: checkpoint keeps used step count\nImplemented: simulated lost response → reconcile one fake receipt\nNot implemented: real provider calls, secure storage, distributed fencing\nNot implemented: hard CPU/memory kill, real auth service, LLM evals"
          },
          {
            "title": "Tradeoffs",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Replacing mocks with real adapters changes the risk boundary. Revisit authentication, transaction behavior, network timeouts, retry layers, receipt lookup, rate limits, isolation and retention. Renaming FakeStore to ProductionStore does not add those controls. In an architecture decision record, describe the next adapter to add and a test that would expose a failure in its contract."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Optional durable adapter exercise",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Run python -m unittest test_durable_state.py from practice. durable_state.py saves versioned JSON to SQLite and uses a conditional update to reject a stale checkpoint writer. Tests reopen the database and use two connections with a controlled sequential interleaving. This is local checkpoint persistence; it does not transact external tool effects, implement leases/fencing, authenticate workers or demonstrate a concurrent multi-process scheduler. Extend with two real processes, lock/busy handling and stale lease tests before claiming multi-worker operation. SQLite power-loss durability also depends on storage and configuration."
            ]
          }
        ],
