        "id": "context",
        "title": "3. Context windows and token budgets",
        "stage": "foundation",
        "takeaway": "Context is the information available for a model decision.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Concept",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Context is the information available for a model decision. It is bounded and may include instructions, conversation, tool definitions and retrieved evidence.",
              "A context window is not unlimited memory. Long histories consume capacity and can obscure the most relevant evidence. Token counts depend on the tokenizer and content; a character count is only an application size bound. Reserve room for the response and required protocol items instead of filling every available input slot. Exact limits and accounting vary by model and provider."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Example",
            "paragraphs": [
              "The next-step package preserves policy, prior searches, versioned evidence and remaining budget without requiring the entire transcript."
            ],
            "example": "Context package for one step:\n  trusted task and tool policy\n  current question\n  explicit state: searched = [\"joins\"]\n  evidence: lesson SQL-07, revision 3, selected excerpt\n  remaining actions: 2\nDiscarding a raw transcript does not justify discarding constraints."
          },
          {
            "title": "Tradeoffs",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Summaries can omit details or introduce errors. Preserve critical constraints and source identifiers in structured state, then review summaries against their sources. Truncating a document mid-sentence can change meaning. The workshop caps characters for deterministic safety but does not call those counts model tokens."
            ]
          }
        ],
        "exercise": {
          "prompt": "A search returns 200 pages. Describe a context selection policy without claiming that all pages fit.",
          "solution": "Bound result count and each excerpt; rank relevant items, preserve IDs and revisions, and fetch narrower evidence if needed. Keep task constraints separately. Record when evidence was omitted, then answer only within available support or ask a narrower question. Use the provider tokenizer/accounting for actual token budgeting.",
          "checks": [
            "Distinguishes characters from tokens.",
            "Preserves provenance and constraints.",
            "Handles insufficient evidence explicitly."
          ],
          "solutionFormat": "prose"
        },
        "quiz": {
          "question": "Does a 4,000-character cap establish a 4,000-token limit?",
          "options": [
            "Yes; every character is one token",
            "Yes; every word always uses four tokens",
            "No; tokenization and character length are different measures"
          ],
          "correct": 2,
          "explanation": "A character cap bounds one payload property; accurate token accounting requires the relevant tokenizer or provider usage data."
        },
