        "id": "clarification",
        "title": "14. Clarify material ambiguity",
        "stage": "intermediate",
        "takeaway": "Ask a focused question when missing intent materially changes the correct result or authorized action.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Concept",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Ask a focused question when missing intent materially changes the correct result or authorized action. Avoid asking for information that safe, relevant evidence can already resolve.",
              "A request to explain joins can be answered generally. A request to change my schedule needs the target schedule and intended change. Offer a small number of meaningful choices and continue independent read-only preparation where appropriate. Needs_input is a legitimate terminal state for the current run, not a failed attempt to appear helpful."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Example",
            "paragraphs": [
              "The two interpretations change different scheduling constraints, so the dependent edit waits for the learner's answer."
            ],
            "example": "User: \"Make my plan faster.\"\nAmbiguity: shorter daily sessions or earlier completion date?\nQuestion: \"Do you want fewer minutes per day, or to finish sooner?\"\nIndependent work: inspect the current plan if authorized\nDependent change: waits for the answer"
          },
          {
            "title": "Tradeoffs",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Too many questions frustrate users, while guessing high-impact intent can cause harm. State any safe assumption explicitly and make it easy to correct. Silence is not approval. A generic yes/no question that hides the actual action is weaker than a concrete preview."
            ]
          }
        ],
