        "id": "methods",
        "title": "Methods and contracts",
        "takeaway": "A method names a small operation with explicit inputs and output.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Parameters and return values",
            "paragraphs": [
              "A method isolates logic so callers do not repeat it. Parameters are local names for supplied values. A return type describes the result; void means no result.",
              "Prefer returning a computed value over printing inside every method. This lets the same logic support a console, a web endpoint and a test. Local functions below work in a top-level Program.cs."
            ],
            "example": "Console.WriteLine(Total(3, 2.50m));\nstatic decimal Total(int quantity, decimal price) => quantity * price;\n// Expected: 7.50 (decimal separator can vary by culture)"
          },
          {
            "title": "Define invalid inputs",
            "paragraphs": [
              "State the method's contract: for example quantity and price must be nonnegative. Validate at the boundary rather than silently producing impossible results.",
              "Parameters are passed by value by default. For a reference type the copied value is a reference, so mutating an object's contents can still affect its caller. Avoid hidden mutation when a return value is enough."
            ],
            "example": "static decimal Total(int quantity, decimal price)\n{\n    if (quantity < 0)\n        throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(quantity), \"Quantity must be nonnegative\");\n    if (price < 0)\n        throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(price), \"Price must be nonnegative\");\n    return quantity * price;\n}\nConsole.WriteLine(Total(2, 4m));\n// Expected: 8"
          },
          {
            "title": "Choose return and failure contracts together",
            "paragraphs": [
              "This is an optional preview of the later integration-checks lab. Complete the small console exercise first; HttpClient adapters and their scripted handler are not foundation requirements.",
              "The focused HTTP client returns Session? because a documented 404 means no session exists. A 503 is not an empty result: it throws, so the caller can show a service problem without falsely claiming the record is absent. A successful response with minutes 0 is valid and must not be confused with missing data.",
              "Its positive-ID precondition is checked before sending. The test counts handler calls and proves an invalid ID dispatches nothing. This combines two observations: the exception and the absence of an external attempt. A test checking only the exception could miss a method that sends first and rejects afterward."
            ]
          }
        ],
