        "id": "recursion",
        "title": "7. Recursion, base cases and trees",
        "takeaway": "A recursive call must move toward a smaller problem.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Concept",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Recursion expresses a problem in terms of smaller instances. A base case stops expansion. A recursive algorithm can still consume substantial stack space even if its code is short.",
              "A tree represents parent-child relationships without cycles. A recursive traversal can process a node and then its children. A highly skewed tree may have depth n; Python recursion has practical limits, so an explicit stack is often safer for deep input."
            ],
            "example": "def total(tree):\n    return tree['minutes'] + sum(total(child) for child in tree['children'])\n\ntree = {'minutes': 10, 'children': [{'minutes': 20, 'children': []}]}\nassert total(tree) == 30"
          },
          {
            "title": "Tradeoffs",
            "paragraphs": [
              "This example assumes a real acyclic tree and well-formed records. A cycle would never reach a base case. Counting calls and maximum depth are different measurements."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Implemented tree practice",
            "paragraphs": [
              "The new binary-search-tree lesson and trees_graphs.py supply an actual search tree; compare its explicit stack with recursive traversal and its height-dependent costs."
            ]
          }
        ],
