        "id": "context-managers",
        "stage": "intermediate",
        "title": "16. Resource lifetimes and cleanup",
        "takeaway": "Context managers bound resource lifetimes and run cleanup even when the body fails.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Design",
            "paragraphs": [
              "with gives a resource a bounded lifetime and exits whether the body succeeds or raises. Files and temporary directories already implement this protocol.",
              "contextlib.contextmanager expresses acquisition before yield and cleanup in finally. Yield exactly once. An exception in the body is delivered at yield; do not accidentally suppress it."
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Example",
            "paragraphs": [
              "The trace records cleanup despite failure, then the outer handler sees the original exception. Prefer a native context manager when the resource already provides one."
            ],
            "example": "from contextlib import contextmanager\ntrace = []\n@contextmanager\ndef resource():\n    trace.append(\"open\")\n    try:\n        yield \"handle\"\n    finally:\n        trace.append(\"close\")\ntry:\n    with resource() as handle:\n        trace.append(handle)\n        raise ValueError(\"failed\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    trace.append(\"caught\")\nprint(trace)\n\n# Expected output:\n# ['open', 'handle', 'close', 'caught']"
          },
          {
            "title": "Tradeoffs",
            "paragraphs": [
              "Catching an exception inside a context manager without reraising can suppress it. Logging alone is not a reason to hide a failure.",
              "Cleanup itself can fail. Prefer existing tested managers and write custom ones when you own a real lifetime rule."
            ]
          }
        ],
        "exercise": {
          "prompt": "Manage a StringIO buffer and prove it closes while preserving an exception raised by its body.",
          "starter": "from contextlib import contextmanager\nfrom io import StringIO\n\n@contextmanager\ndef buffer():\n    stream = StringIO()\n    # TODO: yield stream exactly once and close it in finally.\n    raise NotImplementedError\n\nwith buffer() as normal:\n    normal.write(\"data\")\n    assert normal.getvalue() == \"data\"\nassert normal.closed\n\ntry:\n    with buffer() as failed:\n        failed.write(\"data\")\n        raise ValueError(\"original\")\nexcept ValueError as error:\n    assert str(error) == \"original\"\nelse:\n    raise AssertionError(\"body exception was suppressed\")\nassert failed.closed\nprint(\"cleanup checks passed\")",
          "solution": "from contextlib import contextmanager\nfrom io import StringIO\n@contextmanager\ndef buffer():\n    stream = StringIO()\n    try:\n        yield stream\n    finally:\n        stream.close()\nwith buffer() as normal:\n    normal.write(\"data\")\n    assert normal.getvalue() == \"data\"\nassert normal.closed\ntry:\n    with buffer() as stream:\n        stream.write(\"data\")\n        raise ValueError(\"original\")\nexcept ValueError as error:\n    assert str(error) == \"original\"\nelse:\n    raise AssertionError(\"body exception was suppressed\")\nassert stream.closed\nprint(\"cleanup checks passed\")",
          "checks": [
            "The buffer accepts writes inside with and is closed after a normal exit.",
            "If the body raises ValueError(\"original\"), the same error reaches the caller and the buffer still closes."
          ]
        },
